        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Is Premier Protein or Optimum Nutrition Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Optimum Nutrition is the third-party tested one: Gold Standard 100% Whey Double Rich Chocolate and Vanilla Ice Cream appear in the <a href="https://www.nsfsport.com/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">NSF Certified for Sport</a> listings, and ON describes the line as banned-substance tested. We found no NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport or Informed Choice listing for Premier Protein's powder or shakes, and Premier does not publish one.
          </p>

          <p>
            NSF Certified for Sport is the strictest common certification for supplements. Each certified product is screened for substances banned in sport, checked against its label claims, and made in a facility NSF audits. It is recognized by several major US pro leagues, and it is what drug-tested athletes should look for. ON's own product page lists some sizes as NSF versions, so check for the NSF mark on the tub, since not every flavor and size carries it.
          </p>

          <p>
            No certification does not mean Premier is unsafe. Premier is a large, mainstream brand made under FDA food manufacturing rules, and nothing we found suggests a quality problem. It simply means no independent lab has publicly verified the contents against a banned-substance list or the label. For most people drinking a protein shake for breakfast, that is a small risk. For a college or professional athlete facing drug tests, it is a dealbreaker, and Gold Standard is the clear pick.
          </p>

          <p>
            Neither brand publishes heavy metal test results for these products. If that matters to you, our <a href="/best-nsf-certified-protein-powder/">best NSF certified protein powder</a> guide lists certified tubs, and its notes explain what each certification does and does not test for.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="shakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Premier Protein Shakes vs Optimum Nutrition Shakes: Which Ready-to-Drink Is Better?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Premier Protein's 30 g shake is the better ready-to-drink buy: it gives 30 g of protein per 11 fl oz carton against 24 g in ON's 11 fl oz Gold Standard shake, at almost the same price per 12-pack. Many people searching this comparison mean the cartons, not the tubs, and here the verdict is the opposite of the powder match-up.
          </p>

          <p>
            Per its Amazon listing at the time of writing, the Premier 30 g shake has 160 calories, no added sugar and 24 vitamins and minerals, and our earlier label check found its protein comes mainly from milk protein concentrate and calcium caseinate. Per ON's product page, the Gold Standard shake gives 24 g of protein and 1 g of sugar, with milk protein isolate as the primary ingredient and added zinc plus vitamins A, C, D and E. ON's page text does not give a calorie figure, so read the carton if you are counting.
          </p>

