        <div id="goals" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Which Is Better for Weight Loss, Muscle Gain or a Sensitive Stomach?
          </h2>

          <p>
            For weight loss, the two powders are a tie on the label, because both give about 5 calories per gram of protein with 1 g of sugar, so pick on price and taste. A 30 g serving of either comes to about 150 calories. What helps more with weight loss is using the shake to replace a higher-calorie snack, not adding it on top of your usual meals, and mixing it with water or unsweetened almond milk rather than whole milk.
          </p>

          <p>
            For muscle gain, Gold Standard has the edge, mainly on cost: the cheaper the protein, the easier it is to hit a daily target of about 1.6 g per kg of body weight, the level a large 2018 meta-analysis in the British Journal of Sports Medicine found was enough to maximize strength-training gains for most people. A 5 lb tub of Gold Standard covers far more days than a 17-serving Premier tub. Premier's 30 g serving is convenient post-workout, but you can get the same from 1.25 scoops of ON.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a sensitive stomach, it depends what bothers you. If soy is the issue, Premier is soy-free and Gold Standard contains soy lecithin. If sweeteners bother you, Gold Standard Double Rich Chocolate uses only acesulfame potassium, while Premier uses both sucralose and acesulfame potassium plus xanthan gum and soluble corn fiber, which a few people find gassy. If lactose is the problem, neither is ideal; a pure whey isolate is the better move.
          </p>

          <p>
            A health caveat applies to both: if you have kidney disease, are pregnant, have a milk allergy, take medication, or are an older adult with a medical condition, talk to a doctor or registered dietitian before adding a daily protein shake. Protein powder is a convenient food, not a medicine, and neither brand's products are designed to treat any condition.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="taste" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Which Tastes Better and Mixes Easier, Premier Protein or Optimum Nutrition?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buyers report that both powders taste good and mix easily in a shaker, with Gold Standard winning on flavor choice and Premier winning with people who already drink the Premier cartons. We have not tasted either ourselves; this is a summary of what buyers say in reviews, which is where taste opinions should come from.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gold Standard has the bigger flavor list by far, about two dozen per ON, including Double Rich Chocolate, Extreme Milk Chocolate, Vanilla Ice Cream, French Vanilla Creme, Cookies and Cream, Mocha Cappuccino and seasonal ones. Buyers most often name Double Rich Chocolate and Extreme Milk Chocolate as favorites, and the powder has a long reputation for mixing smoothly with a spoon or shaker. Some buyers say the fruit flavors are weaker than the chocolates. For more options in the same style, see our <a href="/best-chocolate-whey-protein-powder/">best chocolate whey protein powder</a> picks.
          </p>

          <p>
            Premier's powder comes in about five core flavors, including Chocolate Milkshake, Vanilla Milkshake, Cafe Latte and Chocolate Peanut Butter, and buyers often say it tastes much like the bottled shakes, which is the point. The Cafe Latte version adds caffeine, so keep it for mornings. A common buyer note is that the two-scoop serving takes a little more liquid to mix without clumps, so use at least 10 to 12 oz of water or milk.
          </p>

          <p>
            If taste is the deciding factor, the easiest test is cheap: Premier sells small tubs, and ON sells a 0.68 lb Gold Standard tub for about $20 on Amazon. Try a small one of each before committing to a 5 lb tub. And if you want the same head-to-head treatment for two other popular brands, our sibling comparison of <a href="/ryse-vs-ghost-protein/">Ryse vs Ghost protein</a> runs the same checks, as does <a href="/optimum-nutrition-vs-body-fortress/">Optimum Nutrition vs Body Fortress</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing Scoops Instead of Grams</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Premier's 30 g serving is two scoops and 41 g of powder; ON's 24 g serving is one 31 g scoop. Compare protein per gram of powder or cost per 25 g of protein, not per scoop, or the bigger serving will always look like the better deal when it is just more powder.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Judging Value by Tub Price</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Premier's $34.49 tub looks cheap next to ON's $105.99 tub, but the ON tub holds about three and a half times as much protein. At the Amazon prices we saw, the big Gold Standard tub cost about $1.49 per 25 g against Premier's $1.69. Divide price by grams of protein first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming the Powder Matches the Shake</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Premier's powder is a whey isolate and concentrate blend; its 30 g cartons use mainly milk protein concentrate and calcium caseinate with 24 added vitamins and minerals. They taste alike, buyers say, but the labels differ, so read the panel for the one you buy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Uncertified Whey Before a Drug Test</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If you compete in a tested sport, only buy a tub with a current NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport mark. Gold Standard Double Rich Chocolate and Vanilla Ice Cream carry NSF; Premier publishes no certification. Check the mark on your actual tub, since not every size or flavor is listed.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Soy and Sweetener Lines</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Gold Standard contains soy lecithin and Premier does not, while Premier uses both sucralose and acesulfame potassium plus xanthan gum. If either soy or a particular sweetener upsets your stomach, that one line on the ingredient panel matters more than the brand name on the front.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

