                  Premier has more per serving, 30 g against 24 g per the labels at the time of writing, but its serving is two scoops weighing 41 g. Gold Standard has more per gram of powder, about 77% protein by weight against about 73%. Scooped up to 30 g of protein, Gold Standard comes to roughly the same 150 calories as Premier.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Which is cheaper, Premier Protein or Optimum Nutrition?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  At the Amazon prices we saw on 20 November 2026, ON's 5 lb Gold Standard tub cost about $1.49 per 25 g of protein and Premier's 17-serving chocolate tub about $1.69. Small sizes change the picture: ON's 2 lb tub came to about $1.91, and Premier vanilla to about $1.47. Prices move often, so recheck.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Premier Protein third-party tested?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Not by an independent sport certifier that we could find. Premier Protein's powder and shakes do not appear in the NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport or Informed Choice listings, and Premier does not publish third-party test results. Optimum Nutrition's Gold Standard 100% Whey is NSF Certified for Sport in Double Rich Chocolate and Vanilla Ice Cream.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Which shake is better, Premier Protein or Optimum Nutrition?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Premier's. Its 11 fl oz carton gives 30 g of protein and 160 calories per its listing, while ON's 11 fl oz Gold Standard shake gives 24 g. The 12-packs cost almost the same on Amazon, about $26.74 and $26.33, so Premier comes to about $1.86 per 25 g of protein against about $2.29 for ON.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can I use Premier Protein or Gold Standard if I am lactose intolerant?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Possibly, but neither is lactose-free. Both contain whey protein concentrate, which carries some lactose, although isolate is listed first in both. Many people with mild intolerance handle isolate-led blends in a single serving. If you react strongly, choose a pure whey isolate or a plant protein, and ask a doctor or dietitian if symptoms are severe.
                </p>
